
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.zengobi.com/curio/">Curio is a freeform Mac notebook app</a> with a long history and some dedicated users. In September, they released version 34, which adds a new command-line interface with a built-in MCP server for connecting AI assistants.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Now you can take your AI-based research and feed it into Curio for the human part of the work. The new Kanban boards look like a useful way to turn that material into something I can work through visually. If you&#8217;re looking for a way to get a visual representation of your AI data, this is a good option.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">We&#8217;re seeing lots of productivity tools now tie in to AI agents with command-line interfaces and MCP support. <a href="https://www.zengobi.com/curio/cli/">Curio&#8217;s CLI</a> can read projects directly from disk without changing them, or connect to the running app for read and write access. It&#8217;s a separate download for Curio Professional customers with an active subscription or a traditional license within its update window. That distinction lets you choose how far down the rabbit hole you&#8217;re going to go.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;d expect to see more of that from the better productivity apps. While vibe coding is a lot of fun, using a professionally developed app to get your work done is the answer most of us are looking for.</p>
